{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St Peter's is the parish church of Rous Lench, the village named after the Rous family, whose memorials fill a chapel inside. Much of its Norman building survives, and it is listed Grade I. It is the R in the ARCH Benefice.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>A short history</h2>
    <p>The church was built in the middle of the 12th century, and there may have been a church on the site as early as the 9th. Inside, three round Norman arches on sturdy pillars, with scalloped tops, open into the north aisle, and the chancel arch is Norman too.</p>
    <p>In the 1880s the architect Frederick Preedy remodelled the chancel and north aisle and added the north chapel, all in a round-arched style to match the old work. The rose window on the north side and the small bellcote over the chancel arch are his; the bellcote replaced a wooden tower.</p>
    <h2>What to look out for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The south doorway, carved in the first half of the 12th century with zig-zag patterns and spiral columns. Above it sits a figure of Christ seated in majesty, his right hand raised in blessing.</li>
      <li>The Rous family monuments, moved into their own chapel in 1886, among them the canopied tomb of Edward Rous (died 1611), topped by two obelisks.</li>
      <li>Two 17th-century pulpits, brought here from elsewhere.</li>
      <li>Two old bells that are kept rather than hung: one from about 1600 reading "GOD BE OUR SPEDE", the other dated 1661.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
